\par
For Shakespeare\rquote s 17th century audience, this is likely to have been a terrifying scene as it dramatizes the terrible effects that can be wrought on the social fabric by those who seem devoid of morality. The unseating of Prospero from the Dukedom of Milan by his brother, Antonio, may take place before the play opens, but the usurpation of a legitimate sovereign is an act of such terrible consequence that its effects ripple through the play\rquote s action. Antonio\rquote s actions sets a terrible precedent and Sebastian - younger brother of Alonso, the King of Naples - is quick to draw the moral: \ldblquote\'85Thy case, dear friend,/Shall be my precedent; as thou got'st Milan,/I'll come by Naples...\rdblquote  (The Tempest, II.i.50-51). Antonio seems entirely without scruple. When Sebastian asks Antonio if he has any ethical concerns when he is contemplating regicide, the exchange between the two is a significant one:  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Sebastian.\u8239?But, for your conscience?                                                      Antonio.\u8239?Ay, sir; where lies that? if 'twere a kibe, \par
'Twould put me to my slipper: but I feel not \par
This deity in my bosom\'85 \par
\par
 \par
\par
Amoral characters like Antonio stalk early modern Tragedies (Edmund in King Lear; Iago in Othello; Bosola in The Duchess of Malfi): these characters\rquote  lack of interest in ethical matters and the threat that this amorality offers to the established social order is frequently represented on the early modern stage. Antonio is a terrifying figure as moral claims seem to have no purchase on his personal behaviour whatsoever. (Antonio\rquote s silence in the play\rquote s final scene when he is pardoned by Prospero is deafening.). In the above passage Antonio explains to Sebastian that as he will experience no physical pain in killing the King (it is not, after all, as if he were walking with a chilblain \f1\endash  \ldblquote a kibe\rdblquote  - on his foot), the action itself cannot be wrong. So, moral values having been abandoned, what does Antonio put in their place? He replaces ethics with naked self-interest and invites Sebastian to do the same. Significantly, though, he speaks of King Alonso\rquote s death in euphemistic terms \endash  \ldblquote\'85what a sleep were this/For your advancement!...\rdblquote  The brute fact of regicide is replaced by \ldblquote sleep\rdblquote ; ideals of Divine sovereignty and the ethical duties that a subject owes to his King are replaced by ideas of self-\ldblquote advancement\rdblquote . Interestingly, though, despite the ease with which the killing of King Alonso is imagined in this scene (\ldblquote\'85one stroke/Shall free thee from the tribute which thou payest\'85\rdblquote ), the two villains are unable to speak of regicide directly, preferring to deal in euphemisms \endash  the comparison to sleep seems to be reassuring for them \endash  \ldblquote\'85Say, this were death/That now hath seized them; why, they were no worse/Than now they are...\rdblquote  - rather neglecting this is a \ldblquote sleep\rdblquote  from which Alonso and Gonzalo will never awake.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
The way in which human compassion and empathy is replaced with a transactional tendency is exactly reproduced in the next scene in the play (II.ii) when Trinculo encounters Caliban for the first time. Just as Antonio is prepared to ignore Alonso\rquote s humanity so is Trinculo unwilling to acknowledge the humanity of Caliban - \par
\par
 \par
\par
\ldblquote\'85Were I in England now, as once I was, and had but this fish painted, \par
not a holiday fool there but would give a piece of silver...\rdblquote  (II.ii.25-27)\tab  \par
\par
 \par
\par
- instead, Trinculo simply sees the islander (\ldblquote this fish\rdblquote ) as a means of making money. The play seems to dramatize two opposing views of the world: one sees our fellow creatures in purely transactional, or instrumental terms; the other sees the world as a magical, enchanted place where miracles are possible and life is accompanied by wonders (\ldblquote\'85Now, good angels preserve the king\'85\rdblquote ). \par
\par
 \par
\par
Disaster is only averted in this scene because of Prospero\rquote s seeming omniscience: \par
\par
 \par
\par
Ariel.\f0\u8239?My master through his art foresees the danger \par
That you, his friend, are in; and sends me forth\f1\emdash  \par
For else his project dies\emdash to keep them living... \par
\par
 \par
\par
The \ldblquote friend\rdblquote  referred to in Ariel\rquote s speech is, of course, Gonzalo, \ldblquote an honest old councillor\rdblquote , who is to be regarded, I think, as the moral centre of the play. Productions are, significantly, less successful when Gonzalo\rquote s wisdom is subordinated to his credulity: in the Julie Taymor 2010 film version Tom Conti played him \endash  greatly to the film\rquote s detriment \endash  as a blinking idiot. In Conti\rquote s hands Gonzalo was less \ldblquote an honest old councillor\rdblquote  than one who \ldblquote mist[ook] the truth totally\rdblquote  (Sebastian, II.i.55).    \par
\par
 \par
\par
Playing Gonzalo as simply foolish misses the fact that it is he that is favoured by Prospero - and thus awakened by Ariel \endash  while King Alonso, still blind to his good fortune, \ldblquote hear[s] nothing\rdblquote . This scene in the play subtly communicates the play\rquote s moral hierarchy: if Antonio and Sebastian are, despite their noble status, at the bottom, then Gonzalo, The Good Servant, is very definitely at the top.  \par
