\par
    \lquote The Tempest is a play about savagery and its consequences.\rquote   \par
\par
Using your knowledge of the play as a whole, show how far you agree with this view of The Tempest.  \par
\par
Remember to support your answer with reference to different interpretations. [15] \par
\par
 \par
\par
It might be surprising to find savagery in a play that was classified as a Comedy in the First Folio in 1623 by Heminges and Condell, but The Tempest has always been problematic in terms of its genre (is it a Comedy? a Tragi-Comedy? a Pastoral Romance?) and those who find the existence of savagery surprising in an early modern Comedy have obviously never encountered Twelfth Night or any of Ben Jonson\rquote s city comedies.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
Post-colonial criticism has brought savagery in The Tempest into focus, however. Prospero\rquote s terrible threats against Caliban \endash  \par
\par
 \par
\par
\'85thou shalt have cramps,/Side-stitches that shall pen thy breath up\'85 \par
\par
(The Tempest, I.ii.326-327) \par
\par
 \par
\par
\'85thou shalt be pinched/As thick as honeycomb, each pinch more stinging/Than bees that made 'em\'85 \par
\par
(I.ii.329-331) \par
\par
 \par
\par
\'85I'll rack thee with old cramps,/Fill all thy bones with aches, make thee roar/That beasts shall tremble at thy din\'85 \par
\par
(I.ii.369-371) \par
\par
 \par
\par
- tended to be overlooked by the play\rquote s early critics, these words regarded as a straightforward consequence of Caliban\rquote s reluctance to serve his master and the attempted rape of Miranda by the indigenous islander. Octave Mannoni\rquote s text Prospero and Caliban: The Psychology of Civilization (1956) changed the way in which we perceive the violence in this text: Stephen Orgel \endash  one of the play\rquote s most influential editors -sees modern criticism (and, therefore, modern productions) as \ldblquote abandon[ing] traditional representations of Prospero, the serene and detached enchanter\rdblquote . Instead, Prospero the Mage was replaced by a figure whose hunger for power meant the linguistic denigration and physical humiliation of indigenous people: Prospero became the symbol of the boundless greed and unbounded savagery of an extractive capitalism that used notions of western \ldblquote civility\rdblquote  and education as a fig leaf for exploitation. Such savage processes were only possible on the basis that indigenous peoples were \ldblquote vile race[s]\rdblquote  (I.ii.358), incorrigible (Caliban is, according to Prospero \ldblquote A devil, a born devil on whose nature/Nurture can never stick\'85\rdblquote , IV.i.188-189) and therefore unworthy of real consideration.  \par
\par
 \par
\par
The first production on the English stage to be heavily influenced by Mannoni\rquote s seminal work of criticism \endash  the 1970 Mermaid Theatre production, directed by Jonathan Miller \endash  set the \ldblquote savagery\rdblquote  of Prospero in a new context: by casting Black actors as both Caliban and Ariel, Miller depicted  \ldblquote Caliban and Ariel as different forms of black response to white paternalism\rdblquote . According to Orgel, while Caliban responds to Prospero\rquote s \ldblquote savagery\rdblquote  in an adversarial manner and suffers as a consequence, Ariel learns to accommodate Prospero\rquote s demands in such a manner that he finally gains his freedom. In fact, both Ariel and Caliban learn to appease their master, Prospero. Ariel\rquote s claim in Act 1, Scene 2 that he will be \ldblquote\'85correspondent to command/And do my spiriting gently\'85\rdblquote  (I.ii.297-298) has its counterpart in Caliban\rquote s comment in the play\rquote s final scene that \ldblquote\'85I will be wise hereafter,/And seek for grace\'85\rdblquote  (V.i.292-293). If we are all too aware, post-Mannoni, of Prospero\rquote s disquieting relationship with those Europeans whose savagery was a consequence of the colonial process, then we should also note that savage behaviour also characterizes the relationships between the Italians. Antonio and Sebastian are keen to commit regicide in Act 2, Scene 1 and the social fabric proves terribly fragile in the play\rquote s first scene: \par
\par
 \par
\par
Gonzalo.\f0\u8239?Nay, good, be patient. \par
\par
Boatswain.\u8239?When the sea is. Hence! What cares these roarers \par
for the name of king? To cabin: silence! trouble us not. \par
\par
Gonzalo.\u8239?Good, yet remember whom thou hast aboard. \par
\par
Boatswain.\u8239?None that I more love than myself. \par
\par
 \par
\par
This exchange between Gonzalo and the Boatswain is easily missed in the crazed hubbub of the storm, but the Boatswain\rquote s final line here rings the death knell for a culture that is held together with bonds of duty based upon loyalty to the sovereign. As a modern reader (or viewer), it is easy to miss the significance of this early exchange: for us, it is entirely natural that thoughts of self-preservation spring to mind when characters fear destruction. But we have to be reminded, as ever, that \ldblquote The past is a foreign country\rdblquote : the fact that Shakespeare specifically has the Boatswain point out that the natural world cares nothing for social distinctions (\ldblquote\'85What cares these roarers for the name of king?...\rdblquote ) suggests that he is deliberately undermining the foundational bonds of social distinction that underpins early modern European societies. The \ldblquote savagery\rdblquote  of the storm seems to weaken these bonds in such a manner that what is revealed to the audience is a truly savage Hobbesian state of nature where every man is every other man\rquote s enemy. It is no surprise that Prospero\rquote s Magic is required at the end of the play to heal the terrible wounds that have been wrought in The Tempest.   \par
